course: dictionary
lesson: 8070
title: Reliably: can you trust it?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reliably means in a way that can be trusted, every time, without failing. When something works reliably, you can count on it. «The longest reliably measured snakes were about 7 metres, though stories claim far more.» Reliably measured means measured carefully, so the number can be trusted. The stories are not trusted in the same way.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «A credit score is a rating of how reliably someone pays; missing payments lowers it.» The clue is missing payments: a low score means someone does not pay well. So reliably means how well someone keeps up with paying. Try the swap: "a rating of how well someone pays" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: Reliably often goes with words like informed, work and repeat. A reliably informed person was told by someone who can be trusted. In science, a fair test should give results reliably: if you repeat it, you get the same results again.
fact: Reliably means in a way that can be trusted, every time.
fact: «Reliably informed» means told by someone who can be trusted.
fact: «Reliably measured» means measured carefully, so the result can be trusted.
fact: «The dodo was last reliably seen in 1662», so 1662 is the last time people can be sure they saw one.
fact: A fair test should give results reliably: repeating it gives the same results.
exercise: «A bus that runs reliably comes on time.» What does reliably mean here? | Very fast | *Every time, so you can count on it | Late but loud | Once in a while
exercise: «The dodo was last reliably seen in 1662.» What does this suggest? | A made-up story nobody believes | The year the dodo was born | *The last sighting people can trust | Seen by hundreds of sailors
exercise: Which phrase means told by someone who can be trusted? | a shiny snake | work it out | *reliably informed | repeat it
test: Mia says «reliably» means sometimes. Is she right? Why or why not? | No, it means in a way that can be trusted, every time | Yes, it means now and then | Yes, it means she is always late | *No, it means you can count on something every time
test: «Missing payments lowers a credit score and makes borrowing harder.» What does lowers mean? | Makes it higher | Makes it go away | *Makes it go down | Makes it louder
exam: Ben's friend always keeps promises, so people say she acts reliably. What does reliably mean here? | In a way that is hard to notice | *In a way that can be trusted, every time | Very fast and loudly | Only sometimes, when she feels like it
